@section A Register-Machine Simulator

In order to gain a good understanding of the design of register machines, we
must test the machines we design to see if they perform as expected.  One way
to test a design is to hand-simulate the operation of the controller, as in
@ref{Exercise 5.5}.  But this is extremely tedious for all but the simplest
machines.  In this section we construct a simulator for machines described in
the register-machine language.  The simulator is a TypeScript library with four
interface procedures.  The first uses a description of a register machine to
construct a model of the machine (a data structure whose parts correspond to
the parts of the machine to be simulated), and the other three allow us to
simulate the machine by manipulating the model:

@quotation

@example
makeMachine(@var{register-names}, @var{operations}, @var{controller})
@end example

@noindent
constructs and returns a model of the machine with the given registers,
operations, and controller.

@example
setRegisterContents(@var{machine-model}, @var{register-name}, @var{value})
@end example

@noindent
stores a value in a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
getRegisterContents(@var{machine-model}, @var{register-name})
@end example

@noindent
returns the contents of a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
machine.start()
@end example

@noindent
simulates the execution of the given machine, starting from the beginning of
the controller sequence and stopping when it reaches the end of the sequence.
@end quotation

@noindent
As an example of how these procedures are used, we can build a model of the
@abbr{GCD} machine of @ref{5.1.1} as follows.  The controller is data, an
array in the register-machine language of @ref{5.1}, with one constructor
call per line of the machine's diagram:

@example typescript
import @{
  arithmeticOperations,
  assign,
  branch,
  c,
  jump,
  makeMachine,
  mark,
  op,
  reg,
  test,
@} from "@sicp-ts{}/ch5/02-simulator";

const gcdController: ControllerLine[] = [
  mark("test-b"),
  test("=", reg("b"), c(0)),
  branch("gcd-done"),
  assign("t", op("rem", reg("a"), reg("b"))),
  assign("a", reg("b")),
  assign("b", reg("t")),
  jump("test-b"),
  mark("gcd-done"),
];

const gcdMachine = makeMachine(["a", "b", "t"], arithmeticOperations, gcdController);
@end example

@noindent
The first argument to @code{makeMachine} is a list of register names.  The
next argument is the operations table, a record pairing each operation name
with the TypeScript function that implements the operation (that is, produces
the same output value given the same input values); @code{arithmeticOperations}
holds the section's arithmetic, including @code{rem} and @code{=}.  The last
argument specifies the controller as a list of labels and machine
instructions, as in @ref{5.1}.  Assembly can fail, so @code{makeMachine}
returns an @code{Outcome}: an @code{Ok} holding the machine, or a typed
@code{MachineError} naming an unknown register, an undefined or duplicated
label, or an unknown operation.

To compute @abbr{GCD}s with this machine, we set the input registers, start
the machine, and examine the result when the simulation terminates:

@example typescript
import @{ getRegisterContents, setRegisterContents @} from "@sicp-ts{}/ch5/02-simulator";

const machine = expectOk(gcdMachine);
expectOk(setRegisterContents(machine, "a", 206));
expectOk(setRegisterContents(machine, "b", 40));
expectOk(machine.start());
expectOk(getRegisterContents(machine, "a"));
// 2
@end example

@noindent
The helper @code{expectOk} unwraps an @code{Outcome}: it answers the value on
success and lets a fault escape with its rendered message, so a run that
finishes above really finished.  This computation will run much more slowly
than a @code{gcd} procedure written in TypeScript, because we will simulate
low-level machine instructions, such as @code{assign}, by much more complex
operations.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7}Exercise 5.7:} Use the simulator to test the
machines you designed in @ref{Exercise 5.4}.
@end quotation

